Title: THE WELL
Credit: original spec pages
Author: audience-predictor
Draft date: 2026

FADE IN:

EXT. DIRT TRACK - NIGHT

A pickup crawls without headlights. Corn on both sides, taller than the cab. The moon is a dull coin.

INT. PICKUP - NIGHT

MARA (30s) drives one-handed. The other grips a thermos she is not drinking. Beside her, ELLIS (40s) checks a printed map against a dead phone.

ELLIS
It should be after the silo.

MARA
There is no silo.

They pass a silo anyway. Paint peeled to primer. A child's bicycle on its side in the gravel, one wheel still turning.

ELLIS
Don't.

She already stopped.

EXT. FARMHOUSE YARD - NIGHT

The well is a ring of bricks behind the house. No bucket. No roof. A plywood square beside it, nails up.

MARA
You hear that?

A wet tick, like a clock in a drain.

ELLIS
It's the pump.

MARA
There's no power.

He shines a flashlight down. The beam dies ten feet in. Something pale moves at the edge of it, or the mind supplies that.

ELLIS
We're leaving.

MARA
She said she'd be here.

ELLIS
She said a lot of things in August.

INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

Wallpaper swollen with damp. Three bowls on the table, spoons still in them. Mara touches one. Cold. Film on the milk.

A note under a magnet:

    DON'T CALL DOWN.

Ellis finds a second note in his own handwriting, which he does not remember.

    I ALREADY DID.

EXT. THE WELL - NIGHT

Mara on her knees at the brick. She does not call. She listens.

From below, her own voice, a half-second late, repeating the last thing she has not yet said.

MARA
(whisper)
Ellis.

The well answers first.

ELLIS (O.S.)
Don't.

CUT TO BLACK.
